    "body": "## Problem\n\nIn `base64_decode()`, the padding start index `leftover_i` was stored as a signed `int` via `leftover_i = (int)src_i`. When decoding inputs where `src_i >= 2^31`, the cast wraps negative. As a result, `if (leftover_i > -1)` evaluates to `false`, causing padding validation to be skipped. The decoder then accepts invalid input while stopping decoding early, returning an uninitialized heap memory tail in the output buffer.\n\nFixes #42516\n\n## Solution\n\n- Replace the `leftover_i` sentinel with `bool has_padding = false;`. When encountering `'='`, set `has_padding = true; break;`, keeping `src_i` pointing to the first `'='`.\n- Guard padding validation with `if (has_padding)` and iterate using a `size_t` index starting from `src_i`, eliminating the signed `int` cast.\n- Update `padding_len` and `padding_chars` counters from `int` to `size_t`.\n- Audited `base64_encode()` and `base64_decode()`; all indices and lengths now consistently use `size_t`.\n\n### Impact\n\nReal-world impact in Neovim is low because LuaJIT strings are capped just under 2 GiB, so strings $\\ge 2^{31}$ bytes cannot be passed through Lua APIs. This mainly affects direct C callers and PUC Lua builds.\n\n### Testing\n\n- Passed existing functional tests in `test/functional/lua/base64_spec.lua`.\n- Formatted with `make formatc` and passed C linter `make lintc`.\n- Passed commit message validation via `scripts/lintcommit.lua`.\n- A 2 GiB regression test is not included because allocating a $\\ge 2\\text{ GiB}$ buffer is impractical for CI runners and would exceed memory limits.\n\nAI-assisted",
